=head1 NAME

afsio - Introduction to the afsio command suite

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The commands in the B<afsio> command suite provides an interface to interact
with AFS without going through the cache manager, instead operating through
direct calls to the servers.

There are several categories of commands in the B<afsio> command suite:

=over 4

=item *

Commands to interact with files and directories in AFS:
L<B<afsio lock>|afsio_lock(1)>,
L<B<afsio read>|afsio_read(1)>,
L<B<afsio write>|afsio_write(1)>,
and L<B<afsio unlock>|afsio_unlock(1)>.

=item *

Commands to obtain help:
L<B<afsio apropos>|afsio_apropos(1)>
and L<B<afsio help>|afsio_help(1)>.

=item *

A command to display the OpenAFS command suite version: B<afsio version>.

=back

=head1 OPTIONS

The following flag is available on every command in the afsio suite. The
reference page for each command also lists it, but it is described here in
greater detail.

=over 4

=item B<-verbose>

Enables verbose output, including data transfer rates and elapsed time
statistics. Only has function for certain subcommands.

=item B<-clear>

Uses unencrypted (cleartext) connections to file servers. By default,
B<afsio> uses encrypted connections when authenticated.

=item B<-crypt>

Uses encrypted connections to file servers. This is the default when
authenticated.

=item B<-as-user> <I<username>>

Uses localauth credentials to pretend to be the given user, allowing
authenticated RXAFS operations without a running kernel client. This
option is only available on a machine where the local key material
is accessible. The I<username> is a krb4-style name (e.g., C<user.admin>,
not C<user/admin>).

=item B<-help>

Prints a command's online help message on the standard output stream. Do
not combine this flag with any of the command's other options; when it is
provided, the command interpreter ignores all other options, and only
prints the help message.

=back

=head1 PRIVILEGE REQUIRED

The privileges required for afsio commands greatly. Pay attention to the
PRIVILEGE REQUIRED section of each command description.

